  "duration": 121.69000244140625,
  "text": "Hey it's TJ with Hawkeye Cameras. Real quick we're gonna go over a few tips and tricks for transportation and setup of your Hawkeye camera system to and from the field while you've got it on the trap so forth and so on. So the first thing that we're gonna go over is whenever you have the camera mounted next to or near the trap you always want to keep your t-post straight up and down. Hawkeye camera comes with the bracket to mount it on the t-post. You never want to lean your camera over. Now the reason for this is if you lean it over on its side or even flat mount it up high, which we've seen people do that as well, it's possible for water to enter the back of this camera. So keep it straight up and down and use the camera head to angle the video down if you so choose to or need to do that. So one of the more popular places to mount cameras nowadays is up high. Whether it's up high next to a panel trap or all the way up high over the top of a drop trap or a suspended trap, you can use the camera head to angle the video downward. The way that we're gonna do that is remove the dome around the front of this camera. To do that we're gonna do a little counterclockwise twist only about a quarter of an inch and this pops right off. This right here is on a pivot so it can go up or it can go down. So angle that camera head down to however low you need it to go and then you're just gonna pop this back on. There's a little indentation on the bottom of this. You're gonna want to make sure this is pointed downward and it snaps right back on your Hawgye camera system. If you have to force this, push harder than you think, or if it appears difficult to get back on or off, you probably need to take a step back, slow down. You're probably not doing something exactly right and you can damage the little tabs that hold this on there if you force it and break it off. We do sell replacements of these but of course we'd like for you to keep using your original as long as possible.",
